#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81712ebe-0f72-5c06-9717-92c8a5e990d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178240} {:answer/id #uuid "02131d39-657a-5fae-86b5-5750bef35d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178241} {:answer/id #uuid "528d9183-e956-5cff-ac92-38cdee6e4048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178242} {:answer/id #uuid "54ed9dfc-68c4-5f6a-8dd7-666a3b6379cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178243} {:answer/id #uuid "5751ce2f-e67f-53e9-be81-e8d8d3955531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178244}] :answer-field/id #uuid "47f3bda1-6cae-501c-a999-3dbb1502d89d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178239}] :question/id #uuid "e70a7267-ff55-5f84-86c7-c840d8b569be" :question/math-academy-id "q-163491" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x \\ge 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $xm$, the velocity is $vm/s$, and the acceleration is $k(1 - x)m/s^{2}$, where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v = \\sqrt{6}m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v = 3m/s$ when its displacement is $\\frac{3}{2}m$, find the maximum velocity of the particle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "709d94b3-ff84-58ed-80b1-b2c687eaf648" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dde8744-6fdc-5e2d-b736-074a3c935944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365269} {:answer/id #uuid "2e60c10f-bfb0-5906-b010-ebfe71f74caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365270} {:answer/id #uuid "e931fb28-3c65-5cdd-bd58-88282355e37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365271} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1e58b1-199d-5fb2-88e7-6318356fb651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365272} {:answer/id #uuid "59060e22-55d4-5810-9281-aa0ce058e469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e60c10f-bfb0-5906-b010-ebfe71f74caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365270} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4d2807e-e4a2-58a8-bf6a-268785e8822d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "850e5b75-4192-55dd-95c1-60411426a4e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-163550" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of the acceleration is $kx\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $3\\sqrt{2}\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cd/cd42eb74e5bba02951118c349a1b25f7193c39aa53f6504403918dfa984eb2c2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $kx\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-kx.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-kx.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-kx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{2}(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=18-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=3\\sqrt{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =18-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =18-\\frac{k}{2}(3\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ 18-9k & =0 \\\\ 9k & =18 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c102359d-546a-5990-a503-bed5a22df009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365283} {:answer/id #uuid "615e6129-cb6b-5fb5-856e-05f2795d1f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365284} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b12d8b-b0de-57fa-947d-43b59b232996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365285} {:answer/id #uuid "2e32d014-7cfe-590b-89f3-a3bf672b549a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365286} {:answer/id #uuid "7608199a-a3e7-5f54-9204-d6a7d7c0274e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "615e6129-cb6b-5fb5-856e-05f2795d1f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365284} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4de8a78-fdb2-54ab-b4f2-8c9da8d98e67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a4140db-1f5a-5bb9-b5b9-f7487b2da373" :question/math-academy-id "q-163552" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(6-3x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/dd/dd8eecba814fda9c628dc4c8a3853805a31e57f953dd9bf26a6fa79f5c700788.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(6-3x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6-3x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $3>0$ | $0$ | $-3<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6-3x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (6-3x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=6x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =6(0)-\\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =12x-3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}+12x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}+12x+2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(2)=\\sqrt{-3(2)^{2}+12(2)+2}=\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "709d94b3-ff84-58ed-80b1-b2c687eaf648" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b626ce69-c61c-5255-a1e7-d8562cb1f879" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178258} {:answer/id #uuid "bc529b93-24c2-5fa8-823d-ccdef204ec03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178259} {:answer/id #uuid "f435dd66-cff2-5732-a354-e2cc82fdab52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178260} {:answer/id #uuid "18dc80c3-0e6c-532e-bf7b-e4b260c11a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178261} {:answer/id #uuid "48f67410-8402-5642-a39f-18cc678a7261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178262}] :answer-field/id #uuid "152835ac-edaf-5e28-b3f6-2070f61cd97f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178257} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fa7ac9e-782f-536d-a103-0b07454d3bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365324} {:answer/id #uuid "4740ad79-236f-5025-bb6f-e3212b0a30c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365325} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ddac44-8d4a-547e-9fe5-35fb69860c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365326} {:answer/id #uuid "98d268ea-b219-5e3f-97f7-2ae56b03f8ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365327} {:answer/id #uuid "78046bc1-a50a-5de2-9cb0-1b93f74fec8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78046bc1-a50a-5de2-9cb0-1b93f74fec8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365328} :answer-field/id #uuid "55bf7450-4fab-52aa-afa8-517957b4aa80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0e04fbd-7109-56e7-b82c-58a2adaa4ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-163564" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k\\sqrt{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=6\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $9\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b0/b052a65fb0dbcc14a5d359b693beafdf5da3f4bbe1e10f006c315e8168340d5c.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k\\sqrt{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (k\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}(0)^{3/2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+18.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=6\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=9\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+18 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}(9)^{3/2}+18 \\\\ 108 & =18k+18 \\\\ 18k & =90 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "093a897b-2422-5e5b-b713-c8cd61a702dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Particle Is at Instantaneous Rest"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60249e52-7280-5231-8a01-559dd4dcf79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=7\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365345} {:answer/id #uuid "f58cb8b6-b23f-59d0-90a5-c4fa956abc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\sqrt{7}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365346} {:answer/id #uuid "25fbd661-c842-5772-a741-b7693b8d356b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365347} {:answer/id #uuid "262b3b64-1114-5a10-9b30-fa14108cf298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365348} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff31c71-27c0-5899-ac81-5934b4f3e739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25fbd661-c842-5772-a741-b7693b8d356b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\,m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365347} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbc5658e-5cc2-5176-8771-a9a71dd4cfc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31dd5b61-b41b-5d06-a305-866e9e06044a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163572" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $6{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$\nIf $P$ has velocity $2\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $1\\,m,$ find the values of $x$ for which $P$ is instantaneously at rest." :question/worked-solution "![](images/a8/a8c9778580276589ff7a047ca3c7b620de842efd7b3799a70b6e4484bc5fa0e6.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $6{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-2{x}^{3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-2{x}^{3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{7})^{2} & =-2(1)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =14+2 \\\\ C & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=16-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =16-2{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =16-2{x}^{3} \\\\ 2{x}^{3} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =8 \\\\ x & =2\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
